<article class="env-lemma" id="06VB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06VB">Lemma <span data-tag="06VB">46.3.19</span>.</a> Let $A = A_1 \times \ldots \times A_ n$ be a product of rings. An adequate functor over $A$ is the same thing as a sequence $F_1, \ldots , F_ n$ of adequate functors $F_ i$ over $A_ i$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is true because an $A$-algebra $B$ is canonically a product $B_1 \times \ldots \times B_ n$ and the same thing holds for $A$-modules. Setting $F(B) = \coprod F_ i(B_ i)$ gives the correspondence. Details omitted. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>